---
/** Method: source, snapshot and live reading, rating, trend, extension, fairway, water surface, ranking, limits. */
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { srcLink } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { breadcrumbSchema, webPageSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { SEUIL_STABLE } from '../../lib/engine/pegel';
import { FETCHED_AT, RAW_STATIONS } from '../../lib/pegel';
import { quand } from '../../lib/live';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('method', lang);
const title = 'Pegel Heute method: data source, rating, 24-hour trend';
const description = 'How Pegel Heute works: PEGELONLINE raw data, snapshot and live reading, rating against MNW, MW, MHW and marks, 24-hour trend, fairway depth and the limits.';
const s = (k: string, t: string) => srcLink(k, lang, t);
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[webPageSchema({ name: title, description, path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'Method', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Method' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>Method: how Pegel Heute calculates</h1>
    <p>Every tool runs on one set of formulas in your browser. For each part, here is what we do, where the number comes from and where the limit is. Latest snapshot: {quand(FETCHED_AT, lang)}.</p>
    <h2>Source and licence</h2>
    <p>Water levels, gauge datums and reference values come from the <Fragment set:html={s('pegelonline-api', 'PEGELONLINE REST interface')} /> of the Federal Waterways and Shipping Administration. The data is published under the <Fragment set:html={s('dl-de-zero', 'Data licence Germany – Zero – version 2.0')} /> (<Fragment set:html={s('pegelonline-nutzung', 'terms of use')} />). It is unchecked raw data. We show {RAW_STATIONS.length} gauges with a water level on the Rhine, Elbe, Danube, Main, Moselle and Weser.</p>
    <h2>Snapshot and live reading</h2>
    <p>When the site is built, a script queries every station and stores the current reading, the reading from 24 hours earlier and, for twelve stations, 30 days of two-hour means. That is the snapshot in the HTML. When you open a page, your browser asks PEGELONLINE directly: the last 24 hours for the selected station, the current values of all stations on a river in one request for tables, and the last 30 days for curves. Each value says whether it was read live or comes from the snapshot, always with its time (German time).</p>
    <h2>Rating</h2>
    <p>A reading is checked from the top down: at or above the highest level on record (HHW), above flood mark II, above mark I, above mean high water (MHW); then at or below the lowest level on record (NNW), at or below mean low water (MNW); otherwise above or below mean water (MW). Only values PEGELONLINE publishes for the station are used. Without them we write "no official reference values".</p>
    <h2>Trend over 24 hours</h2>
    <p>The trend is the latest reading minus the first reading of the last 24 hours. Below {SEUIL_STABLE} cm of change we write "roughly steady", because waves and measuring noise are of that order. If no reading from 23 hours or more ago exists, we write "trend unknown".</p>
    <h2>"At the same change, reached in"</h2>
    <p>For your comparison level we extend the change of the last 24 hours in a straight line: hours = (your level − current reading) ÷ change over 24 h × 24. If the water is not rising, we write "not in sight". This is not a forecast: flood waves flatten. Forecasts are published by the <Fragment set:html={s('hochwasserzentralen', 'state flood centres')} /> and, for shipping, <Fragment set:html={s('elwis', 'ELWIS')} />.</p>
    <h2>Fairway depth on the Rhine</h2>
    <p>Where PEGELONLINE gives the equivalent low water level (GlW) and the guaranteed fairway depth below GlW (TuGLW), we compute: fairway depth ≈ TuGLW + (reading − GlW). The approximation assumes the water at the shallow spot moves as it does at the gauge. It replaces no sounding and no WSV figure.</p>
    <h2>Water surface above sea level</h2>
    <p>Water surface = gauge datum (PNP, in m above NHN) + reading ÷ 100. The datum and the date it applies from come from PEGELONLINE.</p>
    <h2>Flood marks</h2>
    <p>Marks I and II and their consequences for Rhine shipping follow <Fragment set:html={s('rheinschpv', 'RheinSchPV § 10.01')} />; the values match those in PEGELONLINE. On other rivers we show published marks as a distance, with no legal consequence attached.</p>
    <h2>Ranking</h2>
    <p>To compare stations on different scales we compute the share = (reading − MNW) ÷ (MHW − MNW). 0% is mean low water, 100% mean high water; values below or above are possible. Stations without MNW or MHW are left out of the ranking.</p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>No warning, no forecast, no statement about any property. Federal gauges on six rivers only. Measuring faults and gaps in the raw data are passed on as they are.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
